Given $M$ an $S$-module, $S=Cox(X)$,
\begin{enumerate}
\item can we always find another $S$-module $M'$ satisfying 
\begin{itemize}
\item $\bullet$ $\tilde{M}=\tilde{M}'$
\item $\bullet$ $pdim(M')\geq dim(X)$
\end{itemize}
This is known to be true if $X=\mathbb{P}^{n_1}\times... \times \mathbb{P}^{n_r}$. 

\item What is the relation of this to examples of [Chardin-D'Cruz] where adding embedded components drops regularity?

\item Potential mechanism by truncating $M$ to $(M)_{\geq d}$? (This works for $\mathbb{P}^{n_1}\times... \times \mathbb{P}^{n_r}$'s)
\item Alternatively, could there be an invariant of $\tilde{M}$ which obstructs existence of such an $M'$? 
\end{enumerate}

What can be said about the Rouquier dimension of non-toric varieties and/for their symplectic mirrors? 
\begin{itemize}
\item Toric varieties of dim n $\longleftrightarrow$(mirror) ($T^*T^n$,some stop)
\item What known symplecticly for $(T^*M,stops)$ can be find in (Bai-Cote, Hanlon-Hicks-Lazarev, Favero-Huang)
\end{itemize}
e.g. is there a symplectic $(X^{2n},f)$ such that $Rdim \ Fuk(X,f)\lneq n$? (for instance by finding $(X,f)$ mirror to a singular $Y$ with $Rdim(D^b(Y))>dim(Y)$)

Consider a subspace $S$ of $\mathbb{C}[x]$ of codimension $k$ (defined by $k$ linear constraints, explicitly given). Given a scalar product on $\mathbb{C}[x]$, find an orthogonal basis of $S$ (ordered by degree); some degrees will be missing.
\begin{enumerate}
\item Which degrees get skipped?
\item Characterize these orthogonal polynomials.
\item Does this have anything to do with lacunary Pad\'e approximants?
\end{enumerate}

Let $b \in H^\infty (\Delta)$ with $|| b ||_{H^\infty} \leq 1$. Define $H(b):=(I-T_b T_{\overline{b}})^{1/2} H^2(\Delta)$, where $T_b$ denotes the multiplication operator by $b$. 

\begin{enumerate}

\item When are polynomials dense in $H(b)$? (known in affirmative for non-extremal $b$ or inner $b$) 

\item How to generate the $H(b)$s as reproducing kernel Hilbert spaces to polydisk or the ball in $\mathbb{C}^n$? 

\item Find integral representations of these spaces in setting beyond the unit disk $\Delta$.
\end{enumerate}

Let $f$ be a holomorphic or Hecke--Maass cusp form of level $1$ and $\chi$ be a Dirichlet character of level $p$. For fixed $f$, a level aspect Burgess-type bound for $L(f\times\chi, 1/2)$ can be obtained by (among many others) these two methods: 
\begin{enumerate}
\item Trivial delta method,
\item Using the Kernel $K(z,w) = \sum_{\gamma\in\Gamma_0(p)} \omega(z^{-1}\gamma w)$ for $z, x\in \mathbb{H}$ and $\omega$ a smooth bump function, defined appropriately.
\end{enumerate}
Find a precise relationship between the two methods.
